---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { room, roomNumber, roomUrl } from "../../lib/rooms";
import { OPENZFS_SHA, OPENZFS_TAG } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { COUNTS, REPO_URL, repoFile, VERSIONS } from "../../lib/stack";
import { ANALYTICS_ON } from "../../lib/analytics";

const lang = "en";
const r = room("engine-room");
const tree = `https://github.com/openzfs/zfs/tree/${OPENZFS_SHA}`;
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="engine-room"
  title="How was this museum made? Engine room · Uberblok"
  description="How Uberblok was built: the OpenZFS commit it reads, what the site is made of, the checks that keep every excerpt and source exact, and the repository."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)} · {r.title.en}</p>
      <h1>How was this museum made?</h1>
      <div class="prose intro">
        <p class="lead">
          This page says which OpenZFS code the rooms read, what the site is built with, and how it was written and
          checked.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="code">
      <h2 id="code">The code it reads</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Every room reads OpenZFS <code>{OPENZFS_TAG}</code>, the newest release when the work began, frozen at commit
          <a href={tree}><code class="sha">{OPENZFS_SHA}</code></a>. Every link to the code on this site points to lines
          at that commit, never to a branch, so it still opens on the lines that were read when the code moves on.
        </p>
        <p>
          The excerpts are short quotations of that code, under the
          <a href="https://opensource.org/licenses/CDDL-1.0">Common Development and Distribution License 1.0</a> (CDDL),
          copyright of their authors: Sun Microsystems, Oracle, Delphix, Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory and the
          OpenZFS contributors, as each file’s header states. Each excerpt names its file and lines, and links to them.
          None is retyped: the pages read them at build time from <a href={repoFile("ANALYSIS.md")}><code>ANALYSIS.md</code></a>,
          which holds {COUNTS.excerpts} of them, and a check proves each one identical, byte for byte, to its file at the
          frozen commit. The site does not redistribute OpenZFS files, and its own licences do not cover the excerpts.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="stack">
      <h2 id="stack">What the site is made of</h2>
      <dl class="stack prose">
        <div>
          <dt>Pages</dt>
          <dd>
            <a href="https://astro.build/">Astro</a> {VERSIONS.astro} and TypeScript, built into static HTML: the Hall,
            the History wing, {COUNTS.rooms} rooms and this one, in English, French and Spanish, each at its own address.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Figures</dt>
          <dd>
            SVG and TypeScript written by hand, with no interface framework. Most figures run a small model transcribed
            from the code they show, tested on cases worked out by hand from that code. Apart from a few lines for the
            theme and the language, JavaScript runs only in the figures, and every figure has a text alternative.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>3D</dt>
          <dd>
            <a href="https://threejs.org/">Three.js</a> {VERSIONS.three}, in the Hall and in the two rooms that are
            spatial by nature, the tree and the vdevs. It is loaded only when the 3D view opens, after the 2D figure,
            which stays the fallback without WebGL or with reduced motion.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Code</dt>
          <dd><a href="https://shiki.style/">Shiki</a> {VERSIONS.shiki}, which colours the excerpts at build time.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Type</dt>
          <dd>Fraunces, Inter and Instrument Serif, served with the site.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Hosting</dt>
          <dd>
            {
              ANALYTICS_ON
                ? "Static files on Cloudflare Workers. No server code, no cookies. Cloudflare Web Analytics counts visits without cookies or fingerprinting; to do so, your browser sends Cloudflare the page address, the referrer, the browser type and load times, with your IP address as in any web request. The browser keeps two choices in its local storage, the theme and the language."
                : "Static files on Cloudflare Workers. No server code, no cookies, no analytics: the browser keeps two choices in its local storage, the theme and the language."
            }
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="built">
      <h2 id="built">How it was built</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The site was written by AI agents, from a mission set by a human who approves each release. The
          steps below are how the work was organised.
        </p>
        <ul class="steps">
          <li>
            <strong>The code before the figures.</strong> Before the first figure, <code>ANALYSIS.md</code> mapped the
            architecture and, for each room, the functions and structures that are its idea, with their excerpts. A
            figure that maps to nothing in the code was not drawn.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>One slice at a time.</strong> Fourteen slices, from that analysis to this room, each built end to
            end: the model, the figure, both languages, the checks and the measures.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A review per slice.</strong> A separate QA agent replayed each figure against the code and read the
            captures, and the slice was merged only once approved.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Checks on every change.</strong> The tests of each room’s model; every excerpt and every link to the
            code checked against the frozen commit; every history note checked against its row in
            <a href={repoFile("SOURCES.md")}><code>SOURCES.md</code></a> ({COUNTS.sources} sources); French typography
            checked in the built pages.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>In a real browser.</strong> From the first page on, Playwright captured the pages in both languages, both
            themes, on a phone and a desktop, and walked them with the keyboard alone; Lighthouse measured them.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Decisions in writing.</strong> Every structural choice, with the alternatives set aside, is in
            <a href={repoFile("DECISIONS.md")}><code>DECISIONS.md</code></a>, and every slice, with its measures and
            what it left undone, in <a href={repoFile("BUILDLOG.md")}><code>BUILDLOG.md</code></a>.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="repo">
      <h2 id="repo">The repository</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The sources and these notes: <a href={REPO_URL}>github.com/algotitanhq/uberblok</a>.
          The site’s code is under the MIT licence, its texts and illustrations under
          <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>, and the excerpts under the CDDL, as above.
          Uberblok is not affiliated with the OpenZFS project.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          This is the last room. The <a href={`${roomUrl(lang, "hall")}#map`}>list of all rooms</a> is in the
          Hall.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="engine-room" />
</Base>

<style>
  .room-head {
    padding-top: var(--space-7);
  }
  .room-head h1 {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }
  .intro {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }
  .sha {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .stack {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0;
  }
  .stack > div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 7rem 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-1) var(--space-4);
    padding: var(--space-3) 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .stack dt {
    font-family: var(--font-caption);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--accent);
    line-height: 1.3;
  }
  .stack dd {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .steps {
    padding-left: 1.2em;
  }
  .steps li + li {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }
  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .stack > div {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
